      "Abstract": {
        "CopyrightInformation": "© 2014 John Wiley & Sons A/S. Published by John Wiley & Sons Ltd.",
        "AbstractText": [
          {
            "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
            "Label": "BACKGROUND",
            "content": "Standardization of the hemoglobin A1c (A1c) assay has led to its increasing utilization as a screening tool for the diagnosis of prediabetes and type 2 diabetes in youth. However, significant A1c assay variability remains and has implications for clinical management."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
            "Label": "OBJECTIVE",
            "content": "To describe our center's experiences with A1c results in youth and to evaluate inter-method differences and their clinical implications."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "SUBJECTS",
            "content": "Seventy-five youth (aged 10-18\u2009yr old), body mass index (BMI) ≥85th\u2030 participated."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "METHODS",
            "content": "Seventy-two participants had two A1c values performed on the same sample, one via immunoassay (DCA Vantage Analyzer, A1c1 ) and the other via high performance liquid chromatography (Bio-Rad Variant II, A1c2 ). Nineteen had A1c run on two immunoassay devices (A1c1 and Dimensions Vista, A1c3 )."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
            "Label": "RESULTS",
            "content": "Mean age of participants was 13.9\u2009years, BMI% 97.89%, 33% male, 16% white, 21% black, and 61% Hispanic (H). Mean A1c1 was 5.68%\u2009±\u20090.38 vs. a mean A1c2 of 5.73%\u2009±\u20090.39, p\u2009=\u20090.049. Concordance in diabetes status between methods was achieved in 79% of subjects. Nineteen subjects with A1c3 results had testing performed an average of 22\u2009±\u20099 days prior to A1c1 . Mean A1c3 was 6.24%\u2009±\u20090.4, compared to a mean A1c1 of 5.74%\u2009±\u20090.31, (p\u2009<\u20090.0001). A1c1 was on average systematically -0.5\u2009±\u20090.28 lower compared to A1c3 . There was poor agreement in diabetes classification between A1c1 and A1c3 , with a concordance in classification between methods of only 36.8%."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "content": "Clinically significant inter-method A1c variability exists that impacts patient classification and treatment recommendations. In the screening of obese youth for diabetes, A1c results should be interpreted with caution."
          }
        ]
      },
